{:edb/db-before-t 601 :edb/db-after-t 602 :edb/tx-data [[13194139533914 50 #inst "2026-10-05T12:09:24.148Z" 13194139533914 true] [17592186092110 1020 #edb/ref 17592186265424 13194139533914 true] [17592186092110 1020 #edb/ref 17592186265479 13194139533914 true] [17592186092110 1020 #edb/ref 17592186265484 13194139533914 true] [17592186092110 1020 #edb/ref 17592186265490 13194139533914 true] [17592186092110 1020 #edb/ref 17592186265496 13194139533914 true] [17592186092111 1020 #edb/ref 17592186265452 13194139533914 true] [17592186092111 1020 #edb/ref 17592186265459 13194139533914 true] [17592186092111 1020 #edb/ref 17592186265522 13194139533914 true] [17592186092111 1020 #edb/ref 17592186265527 13194139533914 true] [17592186092111 1020 #edb/ref 17592186265538 13194139533914 true] [17592186092112 1020 #edb/ref 17592186265438 13194139533914 true] [17592186092112 1020 #edb/ref 17592186265445 13194139533914 true] [17592186092112 1020 #edb/ref 17592186265466 13194139533914 true] [17592186092112 1020 #edb/ref 17592186265473 13194139533914 true] [17592186092112 1020 #edb/ref 17592186265517 13194139533914 true] [17592186092118 1020 #edb/ref 17592186265431 13194139533914 true] [17592186092118 1020 #edb/ref 17592186265501 13194139533914 true] [17592186092118 1020 #edb/ref 17592186265506 13194139533914 true] [17592186092118 1020 #edb/ref 17592186265512 13194139533914 true] [17592186092118 1020 #edb/ref 17592186265531 13194139533914 true] [17592186265424 1017 #edb/ref 17592186265425 13194139533914 true] [17592186265424 1089 "q-120887" 13194139533914 true] [17592186265424 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533914 true] [17592186265424 1100 "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions of the equation $4\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2\\sqrt{5}=0$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" 13194139533914 true] [17592186265424 1113 #uuid "46c83a3b-43f7-5f39-a6a6-5d1a9c67c2a6" 13194139533914 true] [17592186265424 1145 "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2\\sqrt{5} & =0 \\\\ 4\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =2\\sqrt{5} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})={60}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${60}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={60}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={60}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={60}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13952163/assets/9c8adbb0a1594ae5bacfab7f1f92993c67659839599e10ab46d5cf98896e3ec7.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={360}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}-{60}^{∘} \\\\  & ={300}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x={60}^{∘},\\,{300}^{∘}.$\n\nFinally, the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ is $60\\cdot 300=18\\,000.$" 13194139533914 true] [17592186265425 1000 #edb/ref 17592186265426 13194139533914 true] [17592186265425 1000 #edb/ref 17592186265427 13194139533914 true] [17592186265425 1000 #edb/ref 17592186265428 13194139533914 true] [17592186265425 1000 #edb/ref 17592186265429 13194139533914 true] [17592186265425 1000 #edb/ref 17592186265430 13194139533914 true] [17592186265425 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533914 true] [17592186265425 1041 "selection" 13194139533914 true] [17592186265425 1104 #edb/ref 17592186265429 13194139533914 true] [17592186265425 1110 #uuid "e7ea14a7-8aed-5635-b543-4a9f8e75932a" 13194139533914 true] [17592186265426 1034 "36\\,000" 13194139533914 true] [17592186265426 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533914 true] [17592186265426 1111 #uuid "9f034b9c-70ae-51d9-87e2-69bb1f7e2537" 13194139533914 true] [17592186265427 1034 "16\\,000" 13194139533914 true] [17592186265427 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533914 true] [17592186265427 1111 #uuid "90128f64-4e01-55e7-8563-6e1f2010bdb7" 13194139533914 true] [17592186265428 1034 "24\\,000" 13194139533914 true] [17592186265428 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533914 true] [17592186265428 1111 #uuid "98513b92-5dfe-5ad3-b2fb-3a68dc71cf2f" 13194139533914 true] [17592186265429 1034 "18\\,000" 13194139533914 true] [17592186265429 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533914 true] [17592186265429 1111 #uuid "830b4ba7-fec4-5ed9-8124-2c7b764d098d" 13194139533914 true] [17592186265430 1034 "12\\,000" 13194139533914 true] [17592186265430 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533914 true] [17592186265430 1111 #uuid "09b5fce7-0be6-5ccb-a14c-0d462241873a" 13194139533914 true] [17592186265431 1017 #edb/ref 17592186265432 13194139533914 true] [17592186265431 1089 "q-120981" 13194139533914 true] [17592186265431 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533914 true] [17592186265431 1100 "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{9}{11}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to one decimal place?" 13194139533914 true] [17592186265431 1113 #uuid "1b6ef5ea-e806-5bce-9fb5-8b166565989c" 13194139533914 true] [17592186265431 1145 "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{9}{11})=0.612\\,554…≈0.612\\,6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $0.612\\,6$ lies inside the specified domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=0.612\\,6.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=0.612\\,6.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=0.612\\,6.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13952163/assets/23712114dae7013de5786e51b99cbd0857c41ed6443e0cb6a9f54aa4ba00dcbb.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -0.612\\,6 \\\\  & ≈5.670\\,6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=0.612\\,6\\cdot 5.670\\,6≈3.5\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place." 13194139533914 true] [17592186265432 1000 #edb/ref 17592186265433 13194139533914 true] [17592186265432 1000 #edb/ref 17592186265434 13194139533914 true] [17592186265432 1000 #edb/ref 17592186265435 13194139533914 true] [17592186265432 1000 #edb/ref 17592186265436 13194139533914 true] [17592186265432 1000 #edb/ref 17592186265437 13194139533914 true] [17592186265432 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533914 true] [17592186265432 1041 "selection" 13194139533914 true] [17592186265432 1104 #edb/ref 17592186265437 13194139533914 true] [17592186265432 1110 #uuid "a33c0a9b-c9dd-5c35-bc30-e81cc341ab7c" 13194139533914 true] [17592186265433 1034 "4.2" 13194139533914 true] [17592186265433 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533914 true] [17592186265433 1111 #uuid "fd9d3da5-1997-5bf3-b3b5-16391da60a2b" 13194139533914 true] [17592186265434 1034 "4.1" 13194139533914 true] [17592186265434 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533914 true] [17592186265434 1111 #uuid "5d669ecf-8ecd-5d2e-b938-90cc3edd9d38" 13194139533914 true] [17592186265435 1034 "3.4" 13194139533914 true] [17592186265435 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533914 true] [17592186265435 1111 #uuid "8ad4ba8b-800f-5955-ba4a-207118545c84" 13194139533914 true] [17592186265436 1034 "3.7" 13194139533914 true] [17592186265436 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533914 true] [17592186265436 1111 #uuid "23a6a3ae-7e93-542d-84bb-64d8fc67838b" 13194139533914 true] [17592186265437 1034 "3.5" 13194139533914 true] [17592186265437 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533914 true] [17592186265437 1111 #uuid "db2b665d-83a5-5f2f-9fe1-51ffb34fa5d3" 13194139533914 true] [17592186265438 1017 #edb/ref 17592186265439 13194139533914 true] [17592186265438 1089 "q-120997" 13194139533914 true] [17592186265438 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533914 true] [17592186265438 1100 "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions of the equation $6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-3\\sqrt{2}=0$ for $-{180}^{∘}<x\\le {180}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" 13194139533914 true] [17592186265438 1113 #uuid "7de16d9d-6113-58f0-8744-682ffcd73ecf" 13194139533914 true] [17592186265438 1145 "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-3\\sqrt{2} & =0 \\\\ 6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =3\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})={45}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${45}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={45}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={45}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={45}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13952163/assets/c722d4fa2b77011e4df5b086c19bb09943446941484cfaf87559ca39a52183bc.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={360}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}-{45}^{∘} \\\\  & ={315}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value is not in the required domain. To get a second solution that's within the required domain, we simply subtract ${360}^{∘}$ from $X:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =X-{360}^{∘} \\\\  & ={315}^{∘}-{360}^{∘} \\\\  & =-{45}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=\\pm {45}^{∘}.$\n\nFinally, the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=45\\cdot (-45)=-2\\,025.\n$$" 13194139533914 true] [17592186265439 1000 #edb/ref 17592186265440 13194139533914 true] [17592186265439 1000 #edb/ref 17592186265441 13194139533914 true] [17592186265439 1000 #edb/ref 17592186265442 13194139533914 true] [17592186265439 1000 #edb/ref 17592186265443 13194139533914 true] [17592186265439 1000 #edb/ref 17592186265444 13194139533914 true] [17592186265439 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533914 true] [17592186265439 1041 "selection" 13194139533914 true] [17592186265439 1104 #edb/ref 17592186265444 13194139533914 true] [17592186265439 1110 #uuid "e657490f-35bc-5404-8f3a-2bde413c1805" 13194139533914 true] [17592186265440 1034 "-2\\,000" 13194139533914 true] [17592186265440 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533914 true] [17592186265440 1111 #uuid "96f6d6e3-0bd3-5882-a560-864cf3b9d86e" 13194139533914 true] [17592186265441 1034 "-4\\,050" 13194139533914 true] [17592186265441 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533914 true] [17592186265441 1111 #uuid "da7cb799-d005-5b30-8db5-96a733a4eb9e" 13194139533914 true] [17592186265442 1034 "-2\\,050" 13194139533914 true] [17592186265442 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533914 true] [17592186265442 1111 #uuid "79be831e-5882-5d90-a89e-9c1c35d3dfce" 13194139533914 true] [17592186265443 1034 "-1\\,050" 13194139533914 true] [17592186265443 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533914 true] [17592186265443 1111 #uuid "02b77fdb-e76e-5852-8333-e57cdd897182" 13194139533914 true] [17592186265444 1034 "-2\\,025" 13194139533914 true] [17592186265444 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533914 true] [17592186265444 1111 #uuid "8db63621-d061-5a45-bad1-c640de376009" 13194139533914 true] [17592186265445 1017 #edb/ref 17592186265446 13194139533914 true] [17592186265445 1089 "q-121001" 13194139533914 true] [17592186265445 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533914 true] [17592186265445 1100 "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions of the equation $\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1=\\frac{3}{4}$ for $-{180}^{∘}<x\\le {180}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" 13194139533914 true] [17592186265445 1113 #uuid "f1b641d9-36cf-5d43-ae0b-f1ea1e2a31f6" 13194139533914 true] [17592186265445 1145 "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1 & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{1}{2})={120}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${120}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={120}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={60}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={60}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13952163/assets/ec1135cb0a4d35130a1c11baf9f421a3cdb4bc22cc1b436686dce722fa3c812e.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={180}^{∘}+{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}+{60}^{∘} \\\\  & ={240}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value is not in the required domain. To get a second solution that's within the required domain, we simply subtract ${360}^{∘}$ from $X:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =X-{360}^{∘} \\\\  & ={240}^{∘}-{360}^{∘} \\\\  & =-{120}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=\\pm {120}^{∘}.$\n\nFinally, the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=120\\cdot (-120)=-14\\,400.\n$$" 13194139533914 true] [17592186265