ratorname{cos}⁡x+1=\\frac{1}{4}$ for $-{180}^{∘}<x\\le {180}^{∘}$ are $x=${{field-1}}${}^{∘}$ and $x=$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" 13194139533914 true] [17592186265473 1113 #uuid "efec1dda-577f-5294-a47d-8a3cd24247de" 13194139533914 true] [17592186265473 1145 "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1 & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-\\frac{3}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{3}{8})=(112.024\\,3…)^{∘}≈{112.024}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${112.024}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution, rounded to the nearest integer, is $x={112}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={67.976}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={67.976}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13952163/assets/18b8161029f2013671daadc367e54e4f02d1d7fac2134ea4953a725236cbddac.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={180}^{∘}+{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}+{67.976}^{∘} \\\\  & ={247.976}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value is not in the required domain. To get a second solution that's within the required domain, we simply subtract ${360}^{∘}$ from $X:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =X-{360}^{∘} \\\\  & ={247.976}^{∘}-{360}^{∘} \\\\  & =-{112.024}^{∘} \\\\  & ≈-{112}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, arranged in ascending order, the solutions are $x={-112}^{∘}$ and $x={112}^{∘}.$" 13194139533914 true] [17592186265474 1000 #edb/ref 17592186265475 13194139533914 true] [17592186265474 1000 #edb/ref 17592186265476 13194139533914 true] [17592186265474 1040