ld-1}} and $x=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139533914 true] [17592186265517 1113 #uuid "22b48a48-cf0d-5b45-afdb-643b245ba807" 13194139533914 true] [17592186265517 1145 "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1 & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-1 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13952163/assets/8ea1a5ca6b0dc0bd7867ab841c629f9966f2b97de4585b9c595a33af206c4a7f.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value is not in the required domain. To get a second solution that's within the required domain, we simply subtract $2\\pi $ from $X:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =X-2\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}-2\\pi  \\\\  & =-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, arranged in ascending order, the solutions are $x=-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ and $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.$" 13194139533914 true] [17592186265518 1000 #edb/ref 17592186265519 13194139533914 true] [17592186265518 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533914 true] [17592186265518 1041 "field-1" 13194139533914 true] [17592186265518 1104 #edb/ref 17592186265519 13194139533914 true] [17592186265518 1110 #uuid "855f1173-5dce-5b3f-a46a-5228eb8bd733" 13194139533914 true] [17592186265519 1034 "-\\frac{3\\pi}{4}" 13194139533914 true] [17592186265519 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533914 true] [17592186265519 1111 #uuid "dff3020a-4098-5421-b2bf-bf2fc390c8e9" 13194139533914 true] [17592186265520 1000 #edb/ref 17592186265521 13194139533914 true] [17592186265520 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533914 true] [17592186265520 1041 "field-2" 13194139533914 true] [17592186265520 1104 #edb/ref 17592186265521 13194139533914 true] [17592186265520 1110 #uuid "ee68c436-f0cf-54a6-9342-7644485d45dd" 13194139533914 true] [17592186265521 1034 "\\frac{3\\pi}{4}" 13194139533914 true] [17592186265521 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533914 true] [17592186265521 1111 #uuid "115e897e-49a3-549b-8afc-b35062c87eee" 13194139533914 true] [17592186265522 1017 #edb/ref 17592186265523 13194139533914 true] [17592186265522 1017 #edb/ref 17592186265525 13194139533914 true] [17592186265522 1089 "q-338738" 13194139533914 true] [17592186265522 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533914 true] [17592186265522 1100 "Arranged in **ascending order**, the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0$ for $-\\pi \\le x<\\pi $ are $x=${{field-1}} and $x=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139533914 true] [17592186265522 1113 #uuid "92118efb-6092-5861-938f-35cfcc896fb3" 13194139533914 true] [17592186265522 1145 "Notice that the solutions to $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0$ correspond to the roots of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ \n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(0)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nThis solution is a quadrantal angle. To find all solutions, we use our knowledge of the cosine function. We know that the cosine function has its roots at\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi .\n$$\n\nTherefore, to find the second solution ${x}_{2}$ in the given range, we set $n=-1$ and we get\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\pi =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions of the equation, arranged in ascending order, are $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" 13194139533914 true] [17592186265523 1000 #edb/ref 17592186265524 13194139533914 true] [17592186265523 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533914 true] [17592186265523 1041 "field-1" 13194139533914 true] [17592186265523 1104 #edb/ref 17592186265524 13194139533914 true] [17592186265523 1110 #uuid "a8c06654-cb97-5bb4-9ae4-62881b7893e8" 13194139533914 true] [17592186265524 1034 "-\\frac{\\pi}{2}" 13194139533914 true] [17592186265524 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533914 true] [17592186265524 1111 #uuid "4923efdd-f65c-5aee-9ea6-c386d4f9856e" 13194139533914 true] [17592186265525 1000 #edb/ref 17592186265526 13194139533914 true] [17592186265525 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533914 true] [17592186265525 1041 "field-2" 13194139533914 true] [17592186265525 1104 #edb/ref 17592186265526 13194139533914 true] [17592186265525 1110 #uuid "82d55449-66ed-5796-abc8-c8c6dcd2ba2d" 13194139533914 true] [17592186265526 1034 "\\frac{\\pi}{2}" 13194139533914 true] [17592186265526 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533914 true] [17592186265526 1111 #uuid "5adb5fa4-e92b-5241-8a37-c99a6451b6fb" 13194139533914 true] [17592186265527 1017 #edb/ref 17592186265528 13194139533914 true] [17592186265527 1089 "q-338744" 13194139533914 true] [17592186265527 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533914 true] [17592186265527 1100 "The solution to the equation $3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-3=0$ for $\\pi \\le x<3\\pi $ is $x=${{field-1}}." 13194139533914 true] [17592186265527 1113 #uuid "67443d1b-c390-5dbe-9010-f3276669865e" 13194139533914 true] [17592186265527 1145 "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-3 & =0 \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =3 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the solutions to $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=1$ correspond to the values of $x$ at which $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ attains its maximum value.\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(1)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $0$ does not lie inside the required domain, we must find other solutions.\n\nThis solution is a quadrantal angle. To find the remaining solutions, we use our knowledge of the cosine function. We know that the cosine function has its maximum values at\n\n$$\nx=2n\\pi .\n$$\n\nTo find a solution in the given range, we set $n=1,$ and we get\n\n$$\nx=2\\pi .\n$$\n\nSince the domain $\\pi \\le x<3\\pi $ is one period, and we have already found one maximum value in this period, we conclude that $x=2\\pi $ is the only solution." 13194139533914 true] [17592186265528 1000 #edb/ref 17592186265529 13194139533914 true] [17592186265528 1000 #edb/ref 17592186265530 13194139533914 true] [17592186265528 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533914 true] [17592186265528 1041 "field-1" 13194139533914 true] [17592186265528 1104 #edb/ref 17592186265530 13194139533914 true] [17592186265528 1110 #uuid "8bfbe3b8-005d-5366-ab11-e52f9f0ddf60" 13194139533914 true] [17592186265529 1034 "\\pi" 13194139533914 true] [17592186265529 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533914 true] [17592186265529 1111 #uuid "c3ade93d-a2f2-5836-abfc-384acec5ff8b" 13194139533914 true] [17592186265530 1034 "2\\pi" 13194139533914 true] [17592186265530 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533914 true] [17592186265530 1111 #uuid "da7731db-8d85-57ff-92cb-ab6a7ea0ffb3" 13194139533914 true] [17592186265531 1017 #edb/ref 17592186265532 13194139533914 true] [17592186265531 1089 "q-72840" 13194139533914 true] [17592186265531 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533914 true] [17592186265531 1100 "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions of the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{1}{4}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ to the nearest integer?" 13194139533914 true] [17592186265531 1113 #uuid "aed35d75-442a-5745-81f9-db85fcbf8be1" 13194139533914 true] [17592186265531 1145 "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{1}{4})=1.318\\,116…≈1.318\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $1.318$ lies inside the specified domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=1.318.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=1.318.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=1.318.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13952163/assets/8797bd2b1558e094b72d891fd344b356e5d579e4549b788bdd0507de23f171c7.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -1.318 \\\\  & =4.965\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{x}_{2}≈1.318\\cdot 4.965≈7\n$$\n\nto the nearest integer." 13194139533914 true] [17592186265532 1000 #edb/ref 17592186265533 13194139533914 true] [17592186265532 1000 #edb/ref 17592186265534 13194139533914 true] [17592186265532 1000 #edb/ref 17592186265535 13194139533914 true] [17592186265532 1000 #edb/ref 17592186265536 13194139533914 true] [17592186265532 1000 #edb/ref 17592186265537 13194139533914 true] [17592186265532 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533914 true] [17592186265532 1041 "selection" 13194139533914 true] [17592186265532 1104 #edb/ref 17592186265536 13194139533914 true] [17592186265532 1110 #uuid "22abdfd4-164a-5e27-bbfa-11c150aab333" 13194139533914 true] [17592186265533 1034 "9" 13194139533914 true] [17592186265533 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533914 true] [17592186265533 1111 #uuid "3f083f61-fe2f-517e-9056-2ae59d95d4aa" 13194139533914 true] [17592186265534 1034 